\lispPackageChapter{livermore/xcs}

The \texttt{livermore/xcs} package is Wilson's XCS~\cite{wilson-1995}, written
from the algorithmic description of Butz and Wilson~\cite{butz-wilson-2002}.
A classifier is a rule $C \to A$ with a prediction, an error, and a fitness.
The system matches the current situation, picks an action, updates the
classifiers that advocated that action, and from time to time runs a genetic
algorithm on the action set rather than on the whole population.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{classifier}

A single rule.  The slots are those of Butz and Wilson:

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{environment-condition}] $C$, a sequence of predicates.
\item[\texttt{action}] $A$, the recommended action.
\item[\texttt{prediction}] $p$, the predicted payoff.
\item[\texttt{prediction-error}] $\epsilon$.
\item[\texttt{fitness}] $F$, used by the genetic algorithm.
\item[\texttt{experience}] $\mathrm{exp}$.
\item[\texttt{time-stamp}] $\mathrm{ts}$.
\item[\texttt{action-set-size}] $\mathrm{as}$.
\item[\texttt{numerosity}] $n$, how many identical copies this macroclassifier
  stands for.
\item[\texttt{accuracy}] $\kappa$.
\end{description}

\lispClassSubsection{xcs}

The running system.  Important slots:

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{learning-parameters}] The parameter set.
\item[\texttt{population}] $[P]$.
\item[\texttt{match-set}] $[M]$.
\item[\texttt{action-set}] $[A]$.
\item[\texttt{previous-action-set}] $[A]_{-1}$.
\item[\texttt{prediction-array}] $[\mathrm{PA}]$.
\item[\texttt{reward}] $\rho$.
\item[\texttt{previous-reward}] $\rho_{-1}$.
\item[\texttt{payoff}] $P$.
\item[\texttt{predicate-type}] The class of alleles, usually
  \texttt{ternary-predicate}.
\item[\texttt{classifier-type}] Usually \texttt{classifier}.
\item[\texttt{situation}] $\sigma$.
\item[\texttt{previous-situation}] $\sigma_{-1}$.
\item[\texttt{action}] $\mathrm{act}$.
\item[\texttt{number-of-situations}] How many situations have been seen.
\item[\texttt{explore?}] True while the current trial is exploring.
\end{description}

\lispClassSubsection{environment}

Presents situations and accepts actions.  Specialize \texttt{get-situation},
\texttt{execute-action}, and \texttt{reset}.  The \texttt{multistep?} slot is
true when a problem lasts for more than one action.

\lispClassSubsection{reinforcement-program}

Scores actions.  Specialize \texttt{get-reward} and \texttt{end-of-problem?}.

\lispClassSubsection{experiment}

Holds an environment, a reinforcement program, and an XCS.  Many problems use
the same object for the first two.

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{number-of-trials}] How many multi-step trials to run.
  Default $10000$.
\end{description}

\section{Match, Action, and Update}

\lispGenericSubsection{generate-match-set}

The ``GENERATE MATCH SET'' function.  $[M]$ is every classifier in $[P]$ that
matches the current situation.  Covering classifiers are added until
$\theta_{\mathrm{mna}}$ distinct actions are present.

\lispGenericSubsection{generate-covering-classifier}

The ``GENERATE COVERING CLASSIFIER'' function.  Builds a classifier whose
condition covers the situation and whose action is a random action missing
from $[M]$.

\lispGenericSubsection{system-prediction}

The fitness-weighted mean prediction of classifiers in $[M]$ that advocate
\lispVar{action}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(system-prediction \lispVar{xcs} \lispVar{action})}

\lispGenericSubsection{select-action-learning}

The ``SELECT ACTION'' function.  With probability $P_{\mathrm{explr}}$ picks a
random action from $[M]$; otherwise picks the action of highest system
prediction.

\texttt{select-action-random} and \texttt{select-action-best} are the two
halves of that choice.

\lispGenericSubsection{generate-action-set}

The ``GENERATE ACTION SET'' function.  $[A]$ is the classifiers in $[M]$ whose
action is the chosen one.  The previous action set is saved first.

\lispGenericSubsection{update-set}

The ``UPDATE SET'' function.  Updates prediction, error, action-set size, and
fitness for the classifiers in an action set, then may run action-set
subsumption.

\lispGenericSubsection{update-fitness}

The ``UPDATE FITNESS'' function.  Accuracy is $1$ when the error is below
$\epsilon_0$, otherwise $\alpha(\epsilon/\epsilon_0)^{-\nu}$.  Fitness then
moves toward the classifier's share of the accuracy sum.

\section{The Genetic Algorithm}

\lispGenericSubsection{run-ga?}

True when the action set is non-empty and the average time since the last
\texttt{GA} exceeds $\theta_{\mathrm{GA}}$.

\lispGenericSubsection{run-ga}

The ``RUN GA'' function.  Selects two parents by fitness, crosses them,
mutates the children, and either subsumes each child into a parent or inserts
it into $[P]$.  Insertion may delete a low-fitness classifier if numerosity
has reached $N$.

\lispGenericSubsection{select-offspring}

The ``SELECT OFFSPRING'' function.  Roulette selection on fitness.

\lispGenericSubsection{crossover}

The ``APPLY CROSSOVER'' function.  Two-point crossover on the condition,
then the midpoint of prediction, error, and fitness.

\lispGenericSubsection{could-subsume?}

The ``COULD SUBSUME'' function.  True when experience exceeds
$\theta_{\mathrm{sub}}$ and error is below $\epsilon_0$.

\lispGenericSubsection{subsume?}

The ``DOES SUBSUME'' function.  True when the actions agree, the general
classifier could subsume, and it is more general.

\lispGenericSubsection{deletion-vote}

The ``DELETION VOTE'' function.  Ranks a classifier for deletion, raising
the vote when experience is high and fitness is a small fraction of the
average.

\lispGenericSubsection{delete-from-population}

The ``DELETE FROM POPULATION'' function.  When total numerosity is at least
$N$, reduces the numerosity of a classifier chosen by deletion vote, or
removes it if numerosity is $1$.

\lispGenericSubsection{insert-into-population}

The ``INSERT IN POPULATION'' function.  If an identical classifier is
already present, its numerosity is increased; otherwise the new classifier
is pushed.

\lispGenericSubsection{do-action-set-subsumption}

The ``DO ACTION SET SUBSUMPTION'' function.  A random most-general subsumer
absorbs every more-specific classifier in the action set.

\section{The Experiment Loop}

\lispGenericSubsection{start}

Starts an experiment.  Multi-step environments use
\texttt{multi-step-experiment}; single-step environments use
\texttt{single-step-experiment}.

\lispGenericSubsection{single-step-experiment}

Runs trials until \texttt{terminate?} is true.  Each trial explores with
probability $P_{\mathrm{explr}}$.

\texttt{single-step-explore} matches, acts, updates, and may run the
\texttt{GA}.  \texttt{single-step-exploit} takes the best action and does not
learn.

\lispGenericSubsection{multi-step-experiment}

Runs \texttt{number-of-trials} problems.  Even-numbered trials (from zero)
explore and learn; odd-numbered trials exploit.

\texttt{multi-step-explore} uses a discounted payoff
$\rho_{-1} + \gamma\max[\mathrm{PA}]$ to update the previous action set.
\texttt{multi-step-exploit} always takes the best action.

\lispGenericSubsection{get-situation}

On an experiment, records the previous situation, reads a new one from the
environment, and advances the situation counter.

\lispGenericSubsection{get-reward}

On an experiment, records the previous reward and reads a new one from the
reinforcement program.

\lispGenericSubsection{terminate?}

True when the experiment should stop.  Specialize this on each experiment
class.

\lispGenericSubsection{single-step-output}

Optional report after a single-step trial.

\section{Identity and Generality}

\texttt{identical?} on two classifiers compares conditions and actions.
\texttt{match?} on a classifier and a situation tests the condition.
\texttt{more-general?} on two classifiers tests the conditions.
\texttt{actions-in} returns the distinct actions in a sequence of
classifiers.
\texttt{duplicate} copies a classifier, including its condition.
